TP.register({id:"lp10-g4",lp:10,grade:"4",mode:"curriculum",checks:9,nextGrade:"5th grade",build(E){const F=E.F;const FLU_TITLE="Setting the Field";const FLU=["The Juniper Bend fair takes over the back field for one afternoon in June, and the field does not give it up easily.","The grass has to be cut twice, once the week before and once on the morning itself, because grass that is cut only once leaves clumps that catch the table legs. Then the ground gets walked. Two people go slowly across the whole field looking for holes, and they mark each one with a wooden stake and a strip of orange tape.","The tables come next, carried out two at a time from the gym. There are fourteen of them. They do not go in a straight line, because a straight line lets a crowd walk past everything without stopping. Instead they go in a wide curve, with the food at the far end, so that people have to pass the student tables to reach it.","By the time the stage is up, the field looks like somebody planned it. Somebody did, back in January."].join("\n\n");const BOX_TITLE="The Cash Box";const BOX=["Ibrahim's job at the fair was the cash box, and he had asked for it. He liked numbers, and he liked being the one who knew the total.","At two o'clock he counted. Ninety-four dollars. He counted again and got ninety-four again, and he wrote it on the card taped to the lid.","At four, when the tables started coming down, he counted a third time and got eighty-six. He went through the box twice more. Eighty-six.","He did not tell anybody for eleven minutes. Then he found Mrs. Okafor at the gate and told her the whole thing, numbers and all, and his voice was not steady.","She asked him one question: had he paid anybody out of the box after two o'clock? He had. Four times, for the lemonade table's cups and ice. She wrote the four amounts down and added them. Eight dollars. Eighty-six and eight is ninety-four."].join("\n\n");const ROOM_TITLE="Give It Room";const ROOM=["Teo had been told to carry the cake table out on his own, and he was no Hercules. He got it as far as the doorway, where one leg caught the frame and the whole thing stopped dead.","“Give it room,” said Freya, without looking up from her clipboard. “Turn it on its side and the legs go through first.”","He turned it. It went through like butter.","For the rest of the morning Freya ran the field with an iron hand. Nobody argued with her, because she had the clipboard and the clipboard had everything on it: who was where, what went where, and when.","By noon she had not sat down once, and the field was a machine."].join("\n\n");const CAKE_TITLE="Why a Cake Rises";const CAKE=["A cake starts out as a thin batter that you could pour. Twenty-five minutes later it is tall and springy. The thing that changes it is gas.","Most cake batters have baking powder in them. Baking powder is a dry white powder that does nothing at all while it sits in the tub. As soon as it is wet, and then warmed, it begins to make carbon dioxide, the same gas that makes the bubbles in soda water.","The gas cannot escape from the thick batter, so it collects in thousands of tiny bubbles and pushes the batter upward. That is the rising.","Heat does the second half of the job. As the oven warms the batter, the flour and the egg firm up around each bubble and hold their shape. Once that happens the cake is set, and it will not fall.","Timing matters. If the oven door is opened before the walls are firm, cool air can shrink the bubbles while the batter is still soft, and the middle of the cake sinks."].join("\n\n");const S1={title:"Bubbles in the Batter",src:"a chapter from the book <i>Kitchen Science at Home</i> by Delia Marchetti, 2021",body:"Baking powder is really two things in one tub: an acid and a base, kept dry so that they cannot touch each other. Water lets them react, and heat speeds the reaction up. One teaspoon can make enough carbon dioxide to raise a whole cake layer."};const S2={title:"Ask a Baker: Why Did My Cake Sink?",src:"a page on the Rosewater Baking School website by Yusuf Carrington, 2023",body:"Nine sunken cakes out of ten come from the same two mistakes. The first is opening the oven door too early. The second is using too much baking powder, which makes bubbles so large that the walls between them tear before the batter has set."};const PAY_READ=["How Juniper Bend Pays for the Fair.","The fair costs about six hundred dollars every year, and the school does not pay for it out of one pocket.","Half of the money comes from a fund the school sets aside in September. The other half is raised during the year, mostly at the spring plant sale.","The rule is simple. Nothing is ordered until the money for it is already in the account.","That is why the fair has never had to cancel anything in the middle of the week, and it is also why the planning team meets in January instead of May."].join("\n\n");const WORDS=["rehearsal","assemble","decorate","admission","generous","individual","temporary","arrangement","contribute","incredible","approximately","souvenir"];const WRITE_DIR="Write on your paper. When you finish, hold it up or read it to your teacher.";const bullets=(...x)=>`<ul class="qlist" style="margin-top:14px">${x.map(s=>`<li>${s}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;const group=(inner,label)=>`<div style="text-align:center">${inner}<p class="fig-label" style="margin:4px 0 0">${label}</p></div>`;const lookBack=(label,inner)=>`<details class="look-back" open><summary>Look back at ${label}</summary>${inner}</details>`;const pills=(...w)=>`<div class="row center" style="margin-top:18px;gap:16px;flex-wrap:wrap">${w.map(x=>`<span class="pill" style="font-size:1.3rem">${x}</span>`).join("")}</div>`;const twoSources=()=>`<div class="texts">${[S1,S2].map((t,i)=>`<article><h3>Source ${i+1}: ${t.title}</h3><p class="small" style="margin:0 0 6px">${t.src}</p><p>${t.body}</p></article>`).join("")}</div>`;const cakeHtml=small=>F.passage(CAKE,CAKE_TITLE,!!small);function noteBoxes(a,b){const bx=t=>`<div style="border:3px dashed var(--line);border-radius:16px;min-width:250px;min-height:100px;padding:8px 16px;background:var(--surface)"><p class="fig-label" style="margin:0;font-size:1.3rem">${t}</p></div>`;return`<div class="figure">${bx(a)}${bx(b)}</div>`}const SPEND=()=>F.table(["What the money paid for","Amount"],[["Paper and printing","$120"],["Food and drink","$240"],["Cloth and ribbon","$90"],["Music and sound","$150"],["<b>Total</b>","<b>$600</b>"]],{caption:"What the fair spent last year"});const SHAPES=[["A",[[12,18],[88,18],[88,82],[12,82]]],["B",[[14,86],[14,20],[80,86]]],["C",[[8,84],[40,22],[92,22],[86,84]]],["D",[[28,28],[96,28],[76,76],[8,76]]],["E",[[12,86],[52,16],[94,70]]]];const shapeRow=()=>`<div class="figure" style="gap:18px;align-items:flex-end;flex-wrap:wrap">${SHAPES.map(s=>group(F.poly(s[1],{size:160,alt:"Flat shape "+s[0]}),"Shape "+s[0])).join("")}</div>`;const STOPS=[{id:1,title:"Read the Field",short:"Read Aloud",parts:[{std:["RF.4.4"],kind:"read",n:0,label:"words read correctly",dur:100,dir:"Read the text out loud. Use your best reading voice.",desc:"a grade-level text read aloud",html:()=>F.passage(FLU,FLU_TITLE),key:["__WC__ words, not counting the title. Time 1 minute and mark errors and self-corrections on your own copy. Let the student finish the passage.","Spring grade 4 benchmark: about 133 words correct per minute (Hasbrouck and Tindal 2017, spring 50th percentile). 25th percentile is about 105. This is the last check of the year, so the spring column is the right one.","Note pace, phrasing, and expression. Listen for a pause at the commas in the long sentence about cutting the grass, and for the short final sentence read as a turn rather than run on from the one before it."]}]},{id:2,title:"Times as Many",short:"Compare",parts:[{std:["4.OA.A.1"],dur:115,dir:"Answer out loud. Write each equation on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"reading a multiplication equation as a comparison and writing comparison statements as equations",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">The fair keeps a tally of everything it sells and everything it uses. Many of the notes are written as comparisons.</p>`,qs:["The lemonade table sold 7 times as many cups of lemonade as cups of tea. It sold 6 cups of tea. How many cups of lemonade did it sell? Write the equation.","Say what 35 = 5 × 7 means as a comparison. Use the words “times as many.”","The banner crew used 48 feet of cloth. That is 8 times as much as the sign crew used. How much did the sign crew use? Write the equation.","Which sentence matches 24 = 4 × 6? A: 24 is 4 more than 6. B: 24 is 4 times as many as 6.","The art class spent $18 on supplies. The music class spent 3 times as much. How much did the music class spend?","A table is 5 feet long. The stage is 45 feet long. The stage is how many times as long as the table?"],key:["42 cups of lemonade. 42 = 7 × 6, or 7 × 6 = 42. Partial: the answer right with no equation written is approaching. (4.OA.A.1, a comparison written as a multiplication equation)","35 is 5 times as many as 7. Accept 35 is 7 times as many as 5. Partial: 35 is 5 more than 7 reads it as an addition and does not count. (4.OA.A.1, reading an equation as a comparison)","6 feet. 48 = 8 × 6, so the sign crew used 6 feet. Partial: 384 multiplies where the problem calls for dividing and does not count. (4.OA.A.1, the unknown is the smaller amount)","B. The word times means the comparison is a multiplication, not an addition. A would be 24 = 6 + 4, which is not true anyway. Partial: choosing B with no reason is approaching. (4.OA.A.1, telling a multiplicative comparison from an additive one)","$54. 54 = 3 × 18. Partial: $21 adds 3 instead of multiplying and does not count. (4.OA.A.1)","9 times as long. 45 = 9 × 5, so the stage is 9 times the length of the table. Partial: 40 feet longer is a difference and not a comparison of times, so it does not count. (4.OA.A.1, the unknown is the multiplier)"]}]},{id:3,title:"Long Word Cards",short:"Word Cards",parts:[{std:["RF.4.3"],kind:"words",words:WORDS,n:12,dur:70,dir:"Read each word out loud. Break it into chunks you already know before you say the whole word.",desc:"decoding unfamiliar multisyllabic words using syllable patterns and word parts",key:["Read each word in order: rehearsal, assemble, decorate, admission, generous, individual, temporary, arrangement, contribute, incredible, approximately, souvenir.","Word parts to listen for: the prefixes re-, ad-, con-, in- (rehearsal, admission, contribute, incredible) and the endings -al, -ate, -ous, -ment, -ible, -ary, -ly (rehearsal, decorate, generous, arrangement, incredible, temporary, approximately).","Watch the syllable breaks: re-hear-sal, as-sem-ble, dec-o-rate, ad-mis-sion, gen-er-ous, in-di-vid-u-al, tem-po-rar-y, ar-range-ment, con-trib-ute, in-cred-i-ble, ap-prox-i-mate-ly, sou-ve-nir.","souvenir is a word borrowed from French: the ou spells the oo sound and the last syllable sounds like neer. Count it correct if the student gets there by chunking even if the first try is off."]}]},{id:4,title:"The Cash Box",short:"Cash Box",parts:[{std:["RL.4.1"],dur:105,dir:"Read the story. Then answer out loud. Use details and examples from the story in every answer.",desc:"referring to details and examples in a story and drawing inferences from them",html:()=>F.passage(BOX,BOX_TITLE,true),qs:["What was Ibrahim's job at the fair, and did he want it? Read the words that tell you.","How much did he count at two o'clock, and how much at four o'clock?","The story never says Ibrahim was frightened. How do you know he was? Give two details.","Why was there less money in the box at four o'clock? Use a detail from the story.","What kind of person is Mrs. Okafor? Give one detail from the story that shows it."],key:["The cash box, and he asked for it. Reads “Ibrahim's job at the fair was the cash box, and he had asked for it,” and “He liked numbers, and he liked being the one who knew the total.” (RL.4.1, explicit details)","Ninety-four dollars at two o'clock and eighty-six dollars at four o'clock. (RL.4.1, explicit details)","Two details: he did not tell anybody for eleven minutes, and his voice was not steady when he told Mrs. Okafor. Counting the box five times in all also counts. (RL.4.1, inference from details)","He had paid money out of the box four times after two o'clock, for the lemonade table's cups and ice, and those four amounts came to eight dollars. Eighty-six and eight is ninety-four, so none of it was lost. (RL.4.1, inference backed by a detail)","Calm and practical, or fair rather than angry. Details: she asked him one question instead of accusing him, and she wrote the amounts down and added them so he could see the total for himself. (RL.4.1, inference with a detail)"]}]},{id:5,title:"Same Amount, Different Pieces",short:"Equal Parts",parts:[{std:["4.NF.A.1"],dur:120,dir:"Look at the bars. Answer out loud and explain how you know. You can write on your paper.",desc:"explaining why two fractions are equivalent and generating equivalent fractions",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">The two bars below are exactly the same size. They stand for two cakes of the same size, cut in two different ways.</p>`,fig:()=>`<div class="figure" style="gap:26px;align-items:flex-end;flex-wrap:wrap">${group(F.fracBar(4,3,{width:320}),"Cake 1, cut into fourths")}${group(F.fracBar(8,6,{width:320}),"Cake 2, cut into eighths")}</div>`,qs:["How much of Cake 1 is shaded? How much of Cake 2? Are the two shaded amounts equal?","Use the picture to explain why 3/4 and 6/8 name the same amount.","Fill in the missing number: 2/3 = ?/12. How did you find it?","Write two fractions that are equal to 1/2.","Is 4/6 equal to 2/3? Show it with multiplication.","Write a fraction equal to 5/10 that has a smaller bottom number."],key:["3/4 of Cake 1 and 6/8 of Cake 2, and yes, the shaded amounts are equal. The bars are the same size and the shading reaches the same place. Partial: both fractions right with no answer about the amounts is approaching. (4.NF.A.1)","Every fourth is cut in half, so there are twice as many pieces and each piece is half as big. 3 fourths becomes 6 eighths, and the amount does not change: 3/4 = (2 × 3)/(2 × 4) = 6/8. Partial: naming the cutting with nothing said about the pieces being smaller is approaching. (4.NF.A.1, explaining equivalence with a visual model)","8/12. The bottom number went from 3 to 12, which is 4 times as many pieces, so the top number has to go up 4 times too: (4 × 2)/(4 × 3) = 8/12. Partial: 8/12 right with no account of how it was found is approaching. (4.NF.A.1)","Any two correct, such as 2/4, 3/6, 4/8, 5/10, 50/100. Partial: one equal fraction given is approaching. (4.NF.A.1, generating equivalent fractions)","Yes. 2/3 = (2 × 2)/(2 × 3) = 4/6. Partial: saying yes with no multiplication shown is approaching. (4.NF.A.1)","1/2. The 5 and the 10 are both divided by 5. Partial: 1/2 right with no dividing shown is approaching. (4.NF.A.1)"]}]},{id:6,title:"Why a Cake Rises",short:"The Cake",parts:[{std:["RI.4.1"],dur:105,dir:"Read the article. Then answer out loud. Use details and examples from the article in every answer.",desc:"referring to details and examples in an informational text and drawing inferences from them",html:()=>cakeHtml(true),fig2:()=>F.row([F.icon("cake",120),"Thin batter in, risen cake out"]),qs:["What gas makes a cake rise? Read the words that tell you.","What does baking powder need before it starts to work? Find the details in the article.","What does the heat of the oven do? Read the sentence that explains it.","The article never says that a cake taken out too early could sink. Use details from the article to explain why it could.","Why does the article call the rising only half of the job? Use a detail."],key:["Carbon dioxide. Reads “it begins to make carbon dioxide, the same gas that makes the bubbles in soda water.” (RI.4.1, explicit detail)","It has to be wet and then warmed. Reads “As soon as it is wet, and then warmed, it begins to make carbon dioxide.” (RI.4.1, explicit details)","It firms up the flour and the egg around the bubbles so they hold their shape. Reads “the flour and the egg firm up around each bubble and hold their shape.” (RI.4.1)","Because the walls would still be soft. The article says the cake is set only once the flour and egg have firmed around the bubbles, and that cool air can shrink soft bubbles, so a cake pulled out before that point has nothing holding the shape up. The article never states this, so the student has to put the details together. (RI.4.1, inference backed by details)","Because the gas only pushes the batter up. Without the heat setting the flour and the egg around the bubbles, nothing would hold the shape. Detail: “Heat does the second half of the job.” (RI.4.1, inference with a detail)"]}]},{id:7,title:"Which Slice Is Bigger?",short:"Compare",parts:[{std:["4.NF.A.2"],dur:110,dir:"Answer out loud and tell how you know. Write the comparisons on your paper.",desc:"comparing fractions with different numerators and denominators, using benchmarks or common denominators",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">The food table writes down how much of each tray is left at the end of the afternoon. Compare them. Every tray is the same size.</p>`,fig:()=>`<div class="figure" style="gap:26px;align-items:flex-end;flex-wrap:wrap">${group(F.fracBar(3,2,{width:300}),"Tray P, 2/3 left")}${group(F.fracBar(4,3,{width:300}),"Tray Q, 3/4 left")}</div>`,qs:["Look at Tray P and Tray Q. Which has more left, 2/3 or 3/4? Show it with a common denominator.","Which is greater, 3/5 or 5/8? Show how you know.","Use 1/2 as a benchmark. Is 5/12 more or less than half a tray?","Put these in order from least to greatest: 1/2, 3/8, 5/6.","Write >, <, or = to make this true: 4/10 ____ 2/5","One tray is small and one tray is large. Pia says 1/2 of the small tray is more than 1/3 of the large tray. Can you settle that by comparing the two fractions? Tell why or why not."],key:["3/4 has more. In twelfths, 2/3 is 8/12 and 3/4 is 9/12, and 9/12 is greater than 8/12. The picture agrees: the shading on Tray Q reaches further. Partial: naming 3/4 with no common denominator shown is approaching. (4.NF.A.2)","5/8 is greater. In fortieths, 3/5 is 24/40 and 5/8 is 25/40. Partial: naming 5/8 with no common denominator shown is approaching. (4.NF.A.2, comparing by making a common denominator)","Less than half. Half of a tray cut into twelfths is 6/12, and 5/12 is less than 6/12. Partial: saying less with no use of 6/12 is approaching. (4.NF.A.2, comparing to a benchmark)","3/8, then 1/2, then 5/6. In twenty-fourths they are 9/24, 12/24, and 20/24. Partial: two of the three in order is approaching. (4.NF.A.2)","4/10 = 2/5. Both are the same amount, because 2/5 = (2 × 2)/(2 × 5) = 4/10. Partial: a greater than or less than sign does not count here, since the two are equal. (4.NF.A.2, recording the result with the equals sign)","No. Two fractions can only be compared when they refer to the same whole. The trays are different sizes, so 1/2 of one is not the same amount as 1/2 of the other, and the fractions alone do not settle it. Partial: saying no with nothing said about the same whole is approaching. (4.NF.A.2, comparisons are valid only when the two fractions refer to the same whole)"]}]},{id:8,title:"Give It Room",short:"Word Work",parts:[{std:["RL.4.4"],dur:105,dir:"Read the story. Then answer out loud. Use the story to explain each answer.",desc:"the meaning of words and phrases in a story, including figurative language and a reference to a figure from mythology",html:()=>F.passage(ROOM,ROOM_TITLE,true),qs:["The story says Teo “was no Hercules.” Hercules is a hero from Greek myths. What is the writer telling you about Teo?","“The whole thing stopped dead.” What does stopped dead mean here?","“It went through like butter.” What kind of comparison is that, and what does it tell you?","Freya “ran the field with an iron hand.” Was her hand really made of iron? What does the phrase mean?","The last line says “the field was a machine.” What does the writer mean by that?"],key:["Hercules was famous for enormous strength, so saying Teo was no Hercules means Teo was not especially strong and the table was too heavy for one person. (RL.4.4, a word or phrase that alludes to a character from mythology)","It stopped suddenly and completely, all at once, with no drifting to a halt. (RL.4.4, meaning of a phrase as used in the text)","A simile, because it uses the word like. It means the table slid through smoothly and easily once he turned it, with nothing catching. (RL.4.4, figurative language)","No. It means she was firmly in charge and did not let anyone change the plan. The story shows it with “Nobody argued with her.” (RL.4.4, figurative language)","A metaphor. It means every part of the field was working together smoothly, each person doing a job at the right time, with nothing wasted. (RL.4.4, figurative language)"]}]},{id:9,title:"Measure the Field",short:"Units",parts:[{std:["4.MD.A.1"],dur:105,dir:"Answer out loud. Fill in the table on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"knowing the relative sizes of measurement units and expressing a larger unit in terms of a smaller one",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">The crew writes every measurement down twice, once in the bigger unit and once in the smaller one, so that nobody has to work it out twice.</p>`,fig:()=>F.table(["Length of a table","In feet","In inches"],[["Short table","1","12"],["Medium table","2","?"],["Long table","3","?"],["Stage front","5","?"]],{caption:"Feet and inches, to copy and finish"}),qs:["Finish the table. How many inches are in 2 feet, in 3 feet, and in 5 feet?","A display table is 2 meters long. How many centimeters is that?","A jug for the lemonade holds 3 liters. How many milliliters is that?","The fair runs for 4 hours. How many minutes is that?","A box of flags weighs 5 kilograms. How many grams is that?","Which is longer, 250 centimeters of ribbon or 3 meters of ribbon? How do you know?"],key:["24 inches, 36 inches, and 60 inches. 1 foot is 12 inches, so multiply the number of feet by 12: 2 × 12 = 24, 3 × 12 = 36, 5 × 12 = 60. Partial: two of the three rows right is approaching. (4.MD.A.1, recording the pairs in a two-column table)","200 centimeters. 1 meter is 100 centimeters, so 2 × 100 = 200. Partial: 20 uses tens instead of hundreds and does not count. (4.MD.A.1)","3,000 milliliters. 1 liter is 1,000 milliliters, so 3 × 1,000 = 3,000. Partial: 300 uses hundreds instead of thousands and does not count. (4.MD.A.1)","240 minutes. 1 hour is 60 minutes, so 4 × 60 = 240. Partial: 240 with no unit named is approaching. (4.MD.A.1)","5,000 grams. 1 kilogram is 1,000 grams, so 5 × 1,000 = 5,000. Partial: 500 uses hundreds instead of thousands and does not count. (4.MD.A.1)","3 meters. Put both in the same unit: 3 meters is 300 centimeters, and 300 is more than 250. Partial: naming 3 meters with no change into the same unit is approaching. (4.MD.A.1, comparing by converting to the smaller unit)"]}]},{id:10,title:"Fix the Fair Notice",short:"Fix It",parts:[{title:"One Mistake Each",std:["L.4.1"],dur:60,dir:"Each line from the fair notice has one mistake. Say the whole line out loud the right way.",desc:"relative pronouns, modal auxiliaries, ordering adjectives, confused words, and run-on sentences",qs:["The booth who sold lemonade ran out of cups.","We should of counted the chairs first.","Their going to set the stage up at eight.","Put these words in the right order: a red big paper banner.","Fix this run-on: The rain stopped the crew carried the tables out."],key:["The booth that sold lemonade ran out of cups. Accept which. A booth is a thing, so it takes that or which, not who. (L.4.1a, relative pronouns)","We should have counted the chairs first. (L.4.1c, modal auxiliaries, and a common usage error)","They're going to set the stage up at eight. Accept They are. (L.4.1g, frequently confused words)","a big red paper banner. Size, then color, then what it is made of, then the noun. (L.4.1d, ordering adjectives within a sentence)","The rain stopped, so the crew carried the tables out. Accept two sentences: The rain stopped. The crew carried the tables out. (L.4.1f, correcting a run-on sentence)"]},{title:"Build a Line",std:["L.4.1"],dur:40,dir:"Answer out loud. Say whole sentences.",desc:"progressive verb tenses, relative adverbs, prepositional phrases, and confused words",qs:["Change this sentence so it tells what was happening: Rosa counts the tickets.","Which word fits? This is the field ____ the fair is held. Is it where or when?","Name the prepositional phrase in this sentence: The cash box sat under the long table.","Which is right? There were too many chairs, or There were to many chairs?"],key:["Rosa was counting the tickets. (L.4.1b, past progressive verb tense)","where (L.4.1a, relative adverb)","The phrase is under the long table, and it tells where the box sat. (L.4.1e, prepositional phrase)","too many chairs. Too means more than enough; to is the little word that goes in front of a place or a verb. (L.4.1g, frequently confused words)"]}]},{id:11,title:"Decimal Ribbons",short:"Decimals",parts:[{std:["4.NF.C.7"],dur:100,dir:"Look at the two grids. Answer out loud and write each comparison on your paper.",desc:"comparing two decimals to hundredths and recording the result with the greater than, less than, or equals sign",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Each grid below is one whole, cut into 100 small squares. The shaded part is written as a decimal.</p>`,fig:()=>`<div class="figure" style="gap:30px;align-items:flex-end;flex-wrap:wrap">${group(F.gridRect(10,10,40,{cell:18}),"Grid 1 shows 0.4")}${group(F.gridRect(10,10,37,{cell:18}),"Grid 2 shows 0.37")}</div>`,qs:["Which is greater, 0.4 or 0.37? Use the grids to explain, then write it with a sign.","Write >, <, or = to make this true: 0.7 ____ 0.70","Put these in order from least to greatest: 0.09, 0.9, 0.19","One ribbon is 0.45 meters long and another is 0.5 meters long. Which ribbon is longer?","Pia says 0.25 is greater than 0.3 because 25 is greater than 3. Is she right? Explain."],key:["0.4 is greater. 0.4 is 40 of the 100 squares and 0.37 is 37 of them, so Grid 1 has more shaded. Written with a sign: 0.4 > 0.37. Partial: naming 0.4 with no sign written is approaching. (4.NF.C.7)","0.7 = 0.70. Seven tenths is the same as seventy hundredths, because each tenth is ten hundredths. Partial: making one of them the greater counts digits instead of places and does not count. (4.NF.C.7)","0.09, then 0.19, then 0.9. In hundredths they are 9, 19, and 90 hundredths. Partial: two of the three in order is approaching. (4.NF.C.7)","The 0.5 meter ribbon. 0.5 is 50 hundredths and 0.45 is 45 hundredths, so 0.5 > 0.45. Partial: naming the ribbon with no reason from hundredths is approaching. (4.NF.C.7)","No. You cannot compare the digits on their own, because the places are different sizes. 0.25 is 25 hundredths and 0.3 is 30 hundredths, so 0.25 < 0.3. Partial: saying no with no count in hundredths is approaching. (4.NF.C.7, comparing by reasoning about the size of the places)"]}]},{id:12,title:"Sort the Sign Shapes",short:"Shapes",parts:[{std:["4.G.A.2"],dur:120,dir:"Look at the five shapes. Answer out loud. Use the corner of a card to test any angle you are not sure about.",mats:["an index card if you have one"],desc:"classifying two-dimensional figures by parallel and perpendicular sides and by angle size, and recognizing right triangles",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">The sign crew cut five flat shapes out of board for the gate. The letter under each one is its name.</p>`,fig:()=>shapeRow(),qs:["Which shapes have at least one pair of parallel sides?","Which shape is a right triangle? How do you know?","Which shape has four right angles? Name two sides of it that are perpendicular.","Shape C has exactly one pair of parallel sides. What is a four-sided shape like that called?","Shape D has two pairs of parallel sides but no right angles. What is that shape called?","Which shape has no parallel sides and no right angle at all?"],key:["A, C, and D. A has two pairs, C has one pair (its top and bottom), and D has two pairs. Partial: two of the three shapes named is approaching. (4.G.A.2, presence of parallel lines)","B. Its upright side and its bottom side meet in a square corner, so it has one right angle, and the slanted side across from that corner is the longest side. Partial: naming B with no reason is approaching. (4.G.A.2, recognizing right triangles)","A. Any two sides that meet, such as the top and the right side, are perpendicular because they meet at a square corner. Partial: naming A with no pair of perpendicular sides given is approaching. (4.G.A.2, perpendicular lines)","A trapezoid. (4.G.A.2, classifying by the presence of parallel lines)","A parallelogram. (4.G.A.2)","E. All three of its angles are smaller than a square corner, and a triangle cannot have parallel sides. Partial: naming E with only one of the two reasons is approaching. (4.G.A.2, classifying by angle size)"]}]},{id:13,title:"Say It Your Way",short:"Listen",parts:[{std:["SL.4.2"],n:5,dur:105,dir:"Listen while your teacher reads. Then say each answer in your own words, not the words you heard.",desc:"paraphrasing parts of a text read aloud and information shown in a table",read:PAY_READ,html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Your teacher will read you a short report about how the school pays for the fair. Listen carefully. Then put what you heard into your own words. After that, look at the table.</p>${SPEND()}`,qs:["Put the first part of the report into your own words. What does the fair cost, and where does the money come from?","Say the school's one rule about spending in your own words.","Put the last part into your own words. Why does that rule matter?","Now look at the table. Say in your own words what the whole table shows.","Pick one row of the table and say it as a sentence of your own."],key:["Paraphrase, not a repeat: the fair costs around six hundred dollars a year, and the money comes from more than one place. Half is put aside in September and half is raised during the year, mostly at the spring plant sale. (SL.4.2, paraphrasing a portion of a text read aloud)","Paraphrase: the school will not order anything until it already has the money for it. (SL.4.2)","Paraphrase: because the money is always there first, the fair has never had to call anything off partway through, and the team can start planning early in the year instead of at the last minute. (SL.4.2)","Paraphrase: the table shows where the six hundred dollars went. Food and drink took the biggest share, and cloth and ribbon the smallest. (SL.4.2, information presented in a different format)","Any row said as the student's own sentence, such as: the fair spent ninety dollars on cloth and ribbon, or printing and paper came to one hundred and twenty dollars. Numbers have to be read off the table correctly. (SL.4.2)"]}]},{id:14,title:"Two Sources",short:"Sources",parts:[{std:["W.4.8"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:100,dir:"Read both sources. Write your notes and your source list on your paper, then hold it up.",desc:"taking notes from two sources, sorting them into categories, and listing the sources",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Here are two more sources about cakes. Take notes from both.</p>${twoSources()}<p class="big">Make two boxes on your paper and label them like the boxes below. Put at least two notes in each box, and write the source number after each note. Then write a list of your two sources at the bottom.</p>${noteBoxes("What makes the gas","What makes a cake sink")}`,key:["Gas box: at least two notes drawn from the sources, such as baking powder holds an acid and a base kept dry (Source 1), water lets them react (Source 1), heat speeds the reaction up (Source 1), one teaspoon can raise a whole layer (Source 1). (W.4.8, gathering relevant information from sources)","Sinking box: at least two notes, such as opening the oven door too early (Source 2), too much baking powder (Source 2), bubbles so large the walls tear before the batter sets (Source 2). (W.4.8, categorizing information)","Notes are brief and in the student's own words or short phrases, not whole sentences copied out. (W.4.8, taking notes)","A list of sources at the bottom naming both: Marchetti, Kitchen Science at Home, 2021; and Carrington, Rosewater Baking School website, 2023. Any clear form counts as long as both are named. (W.4.8, providing a list of sources)"]}]},{id:15,title:"Two Minutes at the Microphone",short:"Report",parts:[{std:["SL.4.4"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:100,dir:"Plan for 1 minute. Then talk for 1 to 2 minutes. Look at the camera and speak clearly.",desc:"a short spoken report on one topic, organized, with facts and descriptive details",html:()=>`<p class="big">At the end of the fair, every class sends one person to the microphone for two minutes.</p><p class="big">Report on one thing you worked on this year. Say what it was, how you went about it, what was hardest, and what you would do differently next time. Use at least four facts or details so that a stranger can picture it.</p>${pills("What it was","How I did it","The hardest part","What I would change")}<p class="small" style="text-align:center;font-size:1.2rem">You can look at the four cards above while you talk.</p>`,key:["Reports on one topic and stays with it for the whole turn, rather than listing several things (SL.4.4)","The report is organized: what it was, then how it was done, then the hard part, then the change, in an order a listener can follow (SL.4.4)","Uses at least four appropriate facts and relevant, descriptive details, such as how long it took, what it was made of, how many there were, or who helped (SL.4.4)","The details support one main idea rather than wandering off it (SL.4.4)","Speaks clearly at an understandable pace, loud enough to hear, and looks at the camera at least some of the time (SL.4.4)"]}]},{id:16,title:"The Fair in Numbers",short:"Place Value",parts:[{title:"Ten Times the Place",std:["4.NBT.A.1"],n:3,dur:70,dir:"Answer out loud. Tell how you know.",desc:"a digit in one place is worth ten times the same digit in the place to its right",html:()=>`<p class="huge" style="text-align:center;letter-spacing:.08em">4,440</p>`,qs:["In this number, what is the 4 in the hundreds place worth, and what is the 4 in the tens place worth? How do the two compare?","What is 700 divided by 70? Say why without doing long division.","Now take the 4 in the thousands place and the 4 in the hundreds place. What is each one worth, and how many times as much is the thousands 4 as the hundreds 4?"],key:["Say: In this number, what is the 4 in the hundreds place worth, and what is the 4 in the tens place worth? How do the two compare? (400 and 40. The hundreds 4 is worth ten times the tens 4, because each place to the left is ten times the one on its right.)","Say: What is 700 divided by 70? Say why without doing long division. (10. 700 is ten times 70, so it takes ten 70s to make 700.)","Say: Now take the 4 in the thousands place and the 4 in the hundreds place. What is each one worth, and how many times as much is the thousands 4 as the hundreds 4? (4,000 and 400. Ten times as much, because the thousands place sits one step to the left of the hundreds place and every place is worth ten times the place on its right. A student who answers 3,600, the difference, has subtracted instead of comparing the places.)"]},{title:"Round the Count",std:["4.NBT.A.3"],n:3,dur:50,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"rounding multi-digit whole numbers to the hundred, the thousand, and the ten",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="text-align:center">The fair keeps a running count. Round in your head and say the number you land on.</p>`,qs:["4,382 people have come through the gate over the years. Round that to the nearest hundred.","Round the same number, 4,382, to the nearest thousand.","The fair has sold 2,748 tickets in five years. Round that to the nearest ten."],key:["Say: 4,382 people have come through the gate over the years. Round that to the nearest hundred. (4,400. 82 is past the halfway mark of 50.)","Say: Round the same number, 4,382, to the nearest thousand. (4,000. 382 is less than 500.)","Say: The fair has sold 2,748 tickets in five years. Round that to the nearest ten. (2,750)","Rate on the place the student rounds to, not just the digit changed. A student who gives 4,400 for the thousand is rounding to the wrong place."]}]},{id:17,title:"Ribbon and Table Top",short:"Decimals Area",parts:[{title:"Tenths and Hundredths",std:["4.NF.C.6"],n:3,dur:55,dir:"Look at the number line. Write your answers on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"writing fractions with denominators 10 and 100 as decimals and placing a decimal on a number line",fig:()=>F.row(F.numberLine({min:0,max:1,step:.1,labelEvery:.1,width:660})),qs:["A ribbon is 62 hundredths of a meter long. Write that length as a decimal.","Write 0.7 as a fraction.","A second ribbon is 0.45 meters. Write that as a fraction of a meter, then point to where 0.45 sits on the number line."],key:["Say: A ribbon is 62 hundredths of a meter long. Write that length as a decimal. (0.62)","Say: Write 0.7 as a fraction. (7/10. Writing 70/100 is also right, and worth a note because it shows the student sees the two as the same amount.)","Say: A second ribbon is 0.45 meters. Write that as a fraction of a meter, then point to where 0.45 sits on the number line. (45/100. It sits exactly halfway between the 0.4 mark and the 0.5 mark.)"]},{title:"Area and Perimeter",std:["4.MD.A.3"],n:3,dur:60,dir:"Look at the table top. Work on your paper. Say your answers out loud.",desc:"applying the area and perimeter formulas for rectangles, including a missing side",fig:()=>F.row(F.rect(7,3,"ft")),qs:["This display table top is 7 feet by 3 feet. What is its area?","What is its perimeter?","A rug covers 48 square feet and is 8 feet long. How wide is it? Tell me how you got it."],key:["Say: This display table top is 7 feet by 3 feet. What is its area? (21 square feet. 7 x 3 = 21. The unit has to be square feet.)","Say: What is its perimeter? (20 feet. 7 + 3 + 7 + 3 = 20, or 2 x (7 + 3) = 20. The unit is plain feet.)","Say: A rug covers 48 square feet and is 8 feet long. How wide is it? Tell me how you got it. (6 feet. Area divided by the known side: 48 divided by 8 = 6, because length times width is the area.)"]}]},{id:18,title:"Two Sources, One Subject",short:"Integrate",parts:[{std:["RI.4.9"],n:3,dur:65,dir:"Read both sources again. Then answer out loud, using both of them.",desc:"integrating information from two texts on the same topic to speak knowledgeably about it",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">These are the same two sources about cakes. This time do not take notes. Put them together and talk.</p>${twoSources()}`,qs:["Using both sources, tell me in two or three sentences. Baking powder makes a cake rise. How? What can make one sink?","Which part of what you just said came from Source 1, and which came from Source 2?","Source 1 says one teaspoon can raise a whole layer. Source 2 says too much powder makes a cake sink. Put those two together. What does that tell a baker about measuring?"],key:["Say: Using both sources, tell me in two or three sentences. Baking powder makes a cake rise. How? What can make one sink? (An answer that holds facts from both: the acid and the base in the powder react once wet and warmed, giving carbon dioxide (Source 1), and a cake sinks from an early oven door or from too much powder tearing the bubble walls (Source 2). One source alone is only half the answer.)","Say: Which part of what you just said came from Source 1, and which came from Source 2? (Names the right source for each fact. This is the check that the student knows the two texts apart.)","Say: Source 1 says one teaspoon can raise a whole layer. Source 2 says too much powder makes a cake sink. Put those two together. What does that tell a baker about measuring? (That a small amount already does the job, so the powder has to be measured carefully, and more is not better. This is a conclusion neither source states on its own.)"]}]},{id:19,title:"Fair Writing",short:"Write",parts:[{alt:{group:"write",key:"W.4.1",label:"Opinion",icon:"💬",hint:"Reasons and information"},std:["W.4.1"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:75,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"a short opinion piece with reasons and supporting information",html:()=>`<p class="big">Should the fair stay on the back field, or move into the gym where the weather cannot reach it? Write your opinion. Write 4 or 5 sentences.</p>${bullets("Open by naming the question and saying what you think","Give two reasons","Back at least one reason with a fact or a detail","Close it off")}`,key:["Introduces the topic and states an opinion clearly (W.4.1)","Supplies at least two reasons for that opinion (W.4.1)","Supports at least one reason with a fact or a detail, such as the grass being cut twice or the tables being carried from the gym (W.4.1)","Provides a concluding statement related to the opinion (W.4.1)"]},{alt:{group:"write",key:"W.4.2",label:"Explain",icon:"📘",hint:"Facts and a definition"},std:["W.4.2"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:75,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"a short informative piece that develops a topic with facts and definitions",html:()=>`<p class="big">Explain to someone who has never been how a school fair gets set up. Write 4 or 5 sentences.</p>${bullets("Open by naming what you are explaining","Group the facts so one idea follows another","Define one word a stranger would not know","Close it off")}`,key:["Introduces the topic clearly in the opening sentence (W.4.2)","Develops the topic with facts, such as cutting the grass twice, staking the holes, fourteen tables, or the curve that slows the crowd (W.4.2)","Defines or explains at least one term a reader would not know, and the facts are grouped rather than listed at random (W.4.2)","Provides a concluding statement (W.4.2)"]},{alt:{group:"write",key:"W.4.3",label:"Story",icon:"✏",hint:"A scene with dialogue"},std:["W.4.3"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:75,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"a short narrative with a clear sequence, dialogue or description, and an ending",html:()=>`<p class="big">Write a short scene about something going wrong at a fair and getting fixed. It can be real or made up. Write 4 or 5 sentences.</p>${bullets("Set it up: who is there and where","Tell the events in an order the reader can follow","Use dialogue or description so the reader can picture it","End it, do not just stop")}`,key:["Orients the reader: establishes a situation and at least one character (W.4.3)","Organizes the events in a sequence that unfolds naturally (W.4.3)","Uses dialogue or descriptive detail to show what happened (W.4.3)","Provides a conclusion that follows from the events (W.4.3)"]}]},{id:20,title:"Goat and Horse",short:"Structures",parts:[{std:["4-LS1-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Look at the two animals. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"arguing that named animal structures do a job that supports survival, growth or reproduction",html:()=>`<p class="big">In the livestock barn at the fair a goat stands on the rail of its pen. A horse waits in the next stall.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("goat",104)}${F.icon("horse",104)}</div>
     <p class="big">The goat has split hooves with soft pads between them, a stomach with four chambers, and eyes with wide oblong pupils. The horse has long legs with a single large hoof, big flat teeth, and ears that turn on their own.</p>`,qs:["Name the job of the goat's split hooves and the job of its four chambered stomach.","Build an argument that the horse's big flat teeth suit what it eats.","The goat's wide oblong pupils let it see nearly all the way round without turning its head. Explain how that supports its survival."],key:["Split hooves with soft pads grip uneven rock and narrow ledges, so the goat can climb and stand where little else can. The four chambered stomach breaks down tough plants that most animals cannot get energy from. (4-LS1-1)","A horse eats grass all day. Grass is tough and gritty, so it needs big flat surfaces to grind it down rather than points for tearing, and large teeth last through years of that wear. Look for the claim plus the reasoning about the food. (4-LS1-1)","An animal that feeds with its head down needs to notice anything approaching from any side. A wide field of view means it can keep eating and still see all round, so it loses less feeding time and is harder to approach unseen. (4-LS1-1)"]},{std:["4-LS1-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read what happens, then answer. You may draw your model.",desc:"modelling the path from sense organ to brain to response in an animal",html:()=>`<p class="big">The horse is dozing in its stall. A balloon pops out on the midway. The horse's ears snap toward the sound, its head comes up, it stands still for a second, then it drops its head and goes back to its hay.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("horse",104)}${F.icon("balloons",104)}</div>`,qs:["Which sense took in the information, and which body part took it in?","The horse stood still and then went back to eating. What was its brain doing?","Build a three step model of the path, and say where the choice was made."],key:["Hearing, taken in by the ears. Turning the ears toward the sound is itself a response that collects better information. (4-LS1-2)","It processed the sound, worked out where it came from and whether it mattered, and chose a response. When nothing followed it chose a different response and went back to eating. Both were decisions. (4-LS1-2)","The ears take in the sound, the brain processes it and chooses, the body carries out the response. The choice was made in the brain, which is the point of this standard. (4-LS1-2)"]}]},{id:21,title:"Digging at the Fairground",short:"Rock Layers",parts:[{std:["4-ESS1-1"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study what the diggers found. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"reading rock layers and the fossils in them as evidence of landscape change",html:()=>`<p class="big">A footing was dug for the new grandstand and four layers showed in the side of the hole. Layers stack with the newest on top.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Depth</th><th>What the layer is</th><th>Fossils in it</th></tr>
     <tr><td>0 to 1 m</td><td>dark field soil</td><td>grass roots, acorns</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1 to 2 m</td><td>river sand with flat layers</td><td>willow leaves and freshwater mussels</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2 to 4 m</td><td>blue gray clay</td><td>oyster shells and crab claws</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4 to 6 m</td><td>sandstone</td><td>sand dollars</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which layer formed first, and how does the chart show that?","The middle layer holds willow leaves and freshwater mussels. What was this spot while that layer formed?","Use all four layers to tell the story of this place from oldest to newest."],key:["The sandstone at 4 to 6 meters, because layers build upward and the ones above landed later. (4-ESS1-1)","A river or a creek. Willows grow on riverbanks and freshwater mussels live in rivers, not in the sea. (4-ESS1-1)","An open sea floor left sandstone with sand dollars, then a salty bay left clay with oysters and crabs, then a freshwater river laid down sand with willow leaves and mussels, then the ground dried out and soil and grass covered it. The salt to fresh to dry sequence is the heart of the answer. (4-ESS1-1)"]},{std:["4-ESS2-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Look at the measurements. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"using measurements to compare the rate of erosion under different conditions",html:()=>`<p class="big">The dirt track at the fairground washes into gullies. Four test strips were laid out on the same slope and the same ten liters of water was run down each one, then the mud at the bottom was weighed.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Strip</th><th>Surface</th><th>Mud weighed</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>bare loose dirt</td><td>310 grams</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>bare dirt rolled hard and smooth</td><td>140 grams</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>a layer of coarse gravel over the dirt</td><td>52 grams</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>grass growing in the dirt</td><td>19 grams</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Put the four surfaces in order from the most eroded to the least, using the numbers.","Rolling the dirt hard cut the loss by more than half. Explain why that works.","Strip 4 lost the least but cannot be used for a racing track. Which surface would you choose for the track, and why?"],key:["Strip 1 at 310 grams, strip 2 at 140 grams, strip 3 at 52 grams, strip 4 at 19 grams. (4-ESS2-1)","Packed grains are pressed tightly together and are heavier to lift, so running water cannot pick them up as easily. Loose grains sit free on the surface and wash away at once. (4-ESS2-1)","Gravel, strip 3, at 52 grams. It cuts the loss to about a sixth of loose dirt and a track can still be used and raked. Any choice is acceptable if it weighs the numbers against the fact that the track has to be used. (4-ESS2-1)"]}]},{id:22,title:"Reading the County Map",short:"Map Patterns",parts:[{std:["4-ESS2-2"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study the chart from the county map. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"interpreting map data to describe patterns in Earth's features",html:()=>`<p class="big">On this map the height lines are drawn every 20 meters. The closer together they are, the steeper the ground.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("map",80)}${F.icon("mountain",80)}${F.icon("tractor",80)}</div>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Part of the county</th><th>Gap between height lines</th><th>What the map shows there</th></tr>
     <tr><td>A, the river flat</td><td>very wide, lines far apart</td><td>fields, the fairground, the town</td></tr>
     <tr><td>B, the low rolling hills</td><td>medium</td><td>orchards and pasture</td></tr>
     <tr><td>C, the canyon sides</td><td>very narrow, lines almost touching</td><td>no roads, a creek at the bottom</td></tr>
     <tr><td>D, the ridge top</td><td>wide again</td><td>a fire road along the crest</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which part of the county is steepest, and how do the height lines tell you?","Parts A and D both have widely spaced lines but one is low and one is high. Explain how both can be fairly flat.","The fairground, the town and the fields are all in part A. Use the map pattern to explain why."],key:["Part C, the canyon sides. Lines almost touching mean the land climbs 20 meters over a very short distance, which is a steep slope. (4-ESS2-2)","Spacing shows steepness, not height. A river flat and a ridge top can both be fairly level ground, one near the bottom and one near the top. Reading the two things separately is the skill here. (4-ESS2-2)","Part A is flat and low beside the river, which is the easiest ground to build on, plow and drive across, and water is close by. The map shows the flat ground and the human features in the same place. (4-ESS2-2)"]},{std:["4-ESS3-2"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Read the two plans. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"generating and comparing solutions that reduce the impact of a natural Earth process",html:()=>`<p class="big">The fairground sits on the river flat, and in a wet winter the lower half goes under water for a week or two. The fair board can fund one plan.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Plan</th><th>What it does</th><th>Cost</th><th>What else it changes</th></tr>
     <tr><td>A: raise the permanent buildings on low piers</td><td>water passes underneath instead of through them</td><td>high</td><td>ramps needed at every door; the ground still floods</td></tr>
     <tr><td>B: move the stalls and pens to the upper half and leave the lower half as a flood meadow</td><td>nothing of value sits in the water when it comes</td><td>low</td><td>a crowded upper half, and the lower half usable only in summer</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Name a third plan of your own for the flooding.","Compare plan A and plan B. Which would you fund. What in the chart supports your choice?","Now suppose the wet winters keep getting worse, so the water comes higher and stays longer. Compare the two plans again on that footing. Which one holds up better, and why?"],key:["Any workable idea: build a low bank around the permanent buildings, raise the ground under the lower half with fill, put in drains that carry the water back to the river as it falls, keep everything at the fairground portable so it can be moved before a storm. (4-ESS3-2)","Either plan, judged on the chart. Plan A keeps the whole site in use and protects the buildings but costs the most and the ground still floods. Plan B costs little and nothing is lost to the water, but the upper half becomes crowded and half the site is seasonal. The reason read from the chart is what is rated. (4-ESS3-2)","B holds up better as the water gets worse, because nothing of value is standing in it and a flood meadow takes a higher and longer flood without anything being lost, while the chart says A leaves the ground flooding and piers are built to one chosen height, so a higher flood reaches the floors they were meant to lift the buildings above. Or: a student who argues for A has to say the piers would be built higher than the worst flood expected, and name the extra cost and the longer ramps that go with it. Partial: a plan named with nothing said about what changes when the water comes higher or stays longer is approaching. (4-ESS3-2)"]}]},{id:23,title:"Power for the Fair",short:"Energy Sources",parts:[{std:["4-ESS3-1"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study the chart. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"describing where energy and fuels come from and how their use affects the environment",html:()=>`<p class="big">Four ways the fair can power its lights, rides and kitchens.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Source</th><th>Natural resource behind it</th><th>Runs out?</th><th>One effect on the environment</th></tr>
     <tr><td>diesel generators behind the midway</td><td>oil pumped from underground</td><td>yes</td><td>exhaust and noise right beside the crowd</td></tr>
     <tr><td>mains power from the town supply</td><td>a mix of natural gas, water behind dams, wind and sunlight</td><td>partly, the gas part does</td><td>gases from the gas part, rivers and land changed by the rest</td></tr>
     <tr><td>propane for the kitchens</td><td>gas from underground</td><td>yes</td><td>gases into the air, tanks trucked in</td></tr>
     <tr><td>solar panels on the exhibition hall roof</td><td>sunlight</td><td>no</td><td>roof space used, panels made and later recycled</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Trace two of these sources back to the natural resource they start from.","The mains power is a mix. Name which part of the mix will run out and which parts will not.","The fair replaces its generators with mains power and roof panels. Name one effect that gets smaller and one new effect that appears."],key:["Any two read correctly: the generators from oil underground, the mains mix partly from natural gas underground and partly from moving water, wind and sunlight, the propane from gas underground, the panels from sunlight. (4-ESS3-1)","The natural gas part will run out, because there is a fixed amount underground. The water behind dams, the wind and the sunlight will not, because rain, wind and sun keep arriving. (4-ESS3-1)","Exhaust and generator noise beside the crowd both get smaller. New effects: the cables and switchgear have to be installed, the panels take roof space and have to be made and recycled, and when the panels give nothing at night the gas part of the mains mix is doing the work. (4-ESS3-1)"]}]},{id:24,title:"Down the Derby Ramp",short:"Speed",parts:[{std:["4-PS3-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Look at the results. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"using evidence to explain how the speed of an object relates to its energy",html:()=>`<p class="big">At the pedal cart derby the same cart was released from three points on the ramp and allowed to roll into a row of foam blocks at the bottom.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Release point</th><th>Speed at the bottom</th><th>Blocks pushed back</th></tr>
     <tr><td>low on the ramp</td><td>2 meters each second</td><td>7 cm</td></tr>
     <tr><td>halfway up</td><td>4 meters each second</td><td>26 cm</td></tr>
     <tr><td>top of the ramp</td><td>6 meters each second</td><td>61 cm</td></tr></table>`,qs:["What happens to the distance the blocks are pushed as the cart's speed goes up?","Which release carried the most energy? Name the measurement that is your evidence.","Explain what this chart shows about the link between an object's speed and its energy."],key:["It grows, and by bigger steps each time: 7 cm, 26 cm, 61 cm. (4-PS3-1)","The release from the top of the ramp, at 6 meters each second, because it pushed the blocks 61 cm, the farthest of the three. (4-PS3-1)","The faster an object is moving, the more energy it carries, and the more it can move something else. The evidence is that each faster run pushed the same blocks farther, and by much larger steps than the speed itself grew. (4-PS3-1)"]},{std:["4-PS3-3"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Answer out loud or in writing. A prediction needs a reason, not the right answer.",desc:"asking a question and predicting the energy change in a collision",html:()=>`<p class="big">Two carts stand at the bottom of the ramp. One is carrying a sack of grain and rolling slowly. The other is empty and still, in its path. Both have foam bumpers on the front.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("tractor",96)}${F.icon("box",96)}</div>`,qs:["Ask one question about this collision that a test could answer.","Predict what happens to each cart after they meet.","The foam bumpers are taken off and the carts meet at the same speed. Predict what is different, and say why."],key:["Any testable question: how far does the empty cart roll, is the bump louder without the bumpers, what if both carts are loaded, does the grain shift in the sack. (4-PS3-3)","The empty cart is pushed forward and rolls away. The loaded cart slows down. Both halves make the full prediction. (4-PS3-3)","The knock is louder and sharper and the carts get a harder jolt, because the foam spread the push out over a longer moment and soaked up some of the energy. Without it, more of the energy goes into sound and into denting the carts in one sudden moment. (4-PS3-3)"]}]},{id:25,title:"Energy Along the Midway",short:"Transfer",parts:[{std:["4-PS3-2"],n:4,dur:75,dir:"For each observation, say how the energy traveled. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"matching observations to energy transferred by sound, light, heat or electric current",html:()=>`<p class="big">Four things noticed along the midway in one evening.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th></th><th>What was noticed</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>The announcer speaks into the microphone and everyone across the field can hear.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>A cable runs from the generator to the ride, and the ride begins to turn.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>The string of bulbs over the midway is switched on and the whole path becomes bright.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>A metal pie tin left on the griddle becomes too hot to pick up.</td></tr></table>`,qs:["How did the energy travel in observation 1?","How did it travel in observation 2?","How did it travel in observation 3?","How did it travel in observation 4?"],key:["By sound, through the air from the loudspeakers across the field to everyone's ears. A student who notices the microphone and cable carry it as an electric current in between is reading it closely. (4-PS3-2)","By an electric current, through the cable from the generator to the ride motor, where it becomes motion. (4-PS3-2)","By light, from the bulbs out along the whole path. (4-PS3-2)","By heat, from the griddle into the metal of the tin. (4-PS3-2)"]},{std:["4-PS3-4"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Draw your device and label it. Then answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"designing, testing and improving a device that converts energy from one form to another",html:()=>`<p class="big">The prize booth wants a spinning wheel that turns by itself when a button is pressed. Design a device that turns electrical energy into the turning of the wheel. You may use a battery, wire, a switch, a small motor, a rubber band belt, and pulleys of different sizes.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("trophy",96)}${F.icon("calculator",96)}</div>`,qs:["Draw and label your device. Show the path from the battery to the wheel turning.","Name the kind of energy going in and the kind coming out.","You test it and the wheel spins far too fast to read the prizes. Name one change and say why it should help."],key:["Any labeled drawing with a path: battery, switch, wire, motor, a belt or pulley, and the wheel. Labels and a complete path through the device make it a design rather than a picture. (4-PS3-4)","Going in: electrical energy from the battery. Coming out: motion of the wheel, plus some sound and heat in the motor. Both forms named. (4-PS3-4)","Any change with a reason: run the belt from a small pulley on the motor to a large pulley on the wheel so the wheel turns more slowly, use a smaller battery so less energy goes in, add a light brake or a drag pad, use a motor built to turn more slowly. (4-PS3-4)"]}]},{id:26,title:"Loudspeakers, Ribbons and Signals",short:"Waves",parts:[{std:["4-PS4-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Look at the two drawings. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"using a wave model to describe amplitude and wavelength and the effect waves have on objects",html:()=>`<p class="big">The sound from the midway loudspeaker was drawn twice: once with the volume set low and once with it set high. The dotted line is silence.</p>
     <div class="figure"><svg viewBox="0 0 520 300" width="520" role="img" aria-label="Two sound waves drawn above one another with letters A and B marking parts of the first wave">
       <text x="10" y="18" font-size="20" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)" font-family="Andika,sans-serif">volume low</text>
       <line x1="20" y1="70" x2="500" y2="70" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="6 6"/>
       <path d="M20 70 q30 -26 60 0 t60 0 t60 0 t60 0 t60 0 t60 0 t60 0 t60 0" fill="none" stroke="var(--sea)" stroke-width="5"/>
       <line x1="50" y1="70" x2="50" y2="57" stroke="var(--coral)" stroke-width="4"/>
       <text x="62" y="63" font-size="22" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)" font-family="Andika,sans-serif">B</text>
       <line x1="20" y1="112" x2="140" y2="112" stroke="var(--coral)" stroke-width="4"/>
       <text x="80" y="134" font-size="22" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)" font-family="Andika,sans-serif" text-anchor="middle">A</text>
       <text x="10" y="186" font-size="20" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)" font-family="Andika,sans-serif">volume high</text>
       <line x1="20" y1="240" x2="500" y2="240" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="6 6"/>
       <path d="M20 240 q30 -84 60 0 t60 0 t60 0 t60 0 t60 0 t60 0 t60 0 t60 0" fill="none" stroke="var(--sea)" stroke-width="5"/>
     </svg></div>`,qs:["Which letter marks the amplitude, and which marks the wavelength?","Which measurement changed when the volume went up, and what does a listener notice?","A paper banner taped near the loudspeaker flutters only when the volume is high. Explain that with the drawings."],key:["B marks the amplitude, from the flat line to the crest. A marks the wavelength, from one crest to the next. (4-PS4-1)","The amplitude grew. In a sound wave a bigger amplitude is heard as a louder sound. The wavelength did not change, so the note is the same pitch. (4-PS4-1)","A bigger amplitude carries more energy, and a wave with enough energy can make an object move. At high volume the wave carries enough to shake the banner; at low volume it does not. (4-PS4-1)"]},{std:["4-PS4-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Answer out loud or in writing. You may draw arrows.",desc:"modelling how light reflects from objects into the eye so the objects can be seen",html:()=>`<p class="big">The prize cups and ribbons are on a shelf under a bright lamp. They shine from across the hall. The hall lights are turned off at the end of the evening. It is completely dark. Nothing on the shelf can be seen.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("trophy",96)}${F.icon("lamp",96)}</div>`,qs:["Draw or tell the path of the light when someone across the hall sees a prize cup. Name each place it goes.","The cups shine and the wooden shelf does not. Explain the difference using the light.","With every light off, nothing on the shelf can be seen. What does that prove about the cups?"],key:["Light leaves the lamp, travels to the cup, reflects off the cup, and travels into the eye. All three legs are needed. (4-PS4-2)","The smooth polished metal sends most of the light back in one direction, so a lot of light arrives at an eye in the right place. The wood scatters the light in all directions and absorbs much of it, so far less reaches any one eye. (4-PS4-2)","They do not make their own light. They only reflect light from the lamp, which is why they vanish when the lamp goes off. (4-PS4-2)"]},{std:["4-PS4-3"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the two systems. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"comparing two solutions that use patterns to send information",html:()=>`<p class="big">The fair needs three messages to reach every stall holder at once: judging starts now, the gates are open, and closing in ten minutes.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>System</th><th>How a message is sent</th><th>Needs</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Bell on the exhibition hall</td><td>one ring, two rings, or a long roll of rings</td><td>heard everywhere, but hard to pick out over a loud midway</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Colored lamp on the flagpole</td><td>a white lamp, a blue lamp, or a flashing lamp</td><td>a clear view of the pole; easiest to see at dusk and after dark</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Say what the pattern is in each system.","It is noon and the midway is loud. Which system works better, and what in the chart tells you?","Design a third system of your own for the same three messages, using neither a bell nor a lamp."],key:["In the bell system the pattern is the number of rings and how long the ringing lasts. In the lamp system it is which color shows and whether it is steady or flashing. An agreed pattern standing for a message is what carries the information. (4-PS4-3)","The lamp. The chart says the bell is hard to pick out over a loud midway, while the lamp needs only a clear view of the pole. A student who notes that the lamp is harder to see at noon than at dusk is reading closely and should get credit for the trade off. (4-PS4-3)","Any pattern with three agreed signals: a flag raised to one of three heights, a board with one, two or three shapes on it, three agreed words over the loudspeaker, a banner turned to one of three colors, one, two or three pennants on the pole. (4-PS4-3)"]}]},{id:27,title:"A Canopy That Stays Up",short:"Design",parts:[{std:["3-5-ETS1-1"],n:3,dur:60,dir:"Read the situation, then answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"defining a design problem with criteria for success and constraints",html:()=>`<p class="big">The shade canopy over the lemonade stall lifts and tips whenever the afternoon wind comes up. It has to be held down by hand. The crew has one morning, the canopy frame and cloth, rope, tent pegs, and four buckets they can fill with sand.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("tent",88)}${F.icon("wind",88)}</div>`,qs:["State the problem in one sentence, in your own words.","Write two criteria that would tell the crew the fix works.","Write two constraints from the situation."],key:["Something like: the canopy needs to stay standing and in place in the afternoon wind without anybody holding it. A problem statement, not a solution. (3-5-ETS1-1)","Any two testable criteria: it stays up through an afternoon with no hands on it, it does not shift more than a few centimeters, it still shades the counter, two people can set it up in ten minutes. (3-5-ETS1-1)","One morning, only the frame and cloth, rope, pegs and four sand buckets. Any two. A criterion written here instead is approaching. (3-5-ETS1-1)"]},{std:["3-5-ETS1-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Study the three designs. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"generating and comparing solutions against the criteria and constraints",html:()=>`<p class="big">Three fixes for the canopy.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Fix</th><th>What it is</th><th>Materials</th><th>Set up time</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>a sand bucket tied to each leg</td><td>buckets, rope</td><td>short</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>guy ropes from the top of each leg out to pegs in the ground</td><td>rope and pegs</td><td>short</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>a steel framed canopy bolted into a concrete pad</td><td>new frame and concrete to buy</td><td>two weeks</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which fix breaks a constraint? Name it.","Compare fixes 1 and 2 against the criteria. Which do you expect to hold better in a strong gust, and why?","Add a fourth idea of your own using only what is on hand."],key:["Fix 3. The frame and concrete have to be bought and two weeks is far longer than the one morning available. (3-5-ETS1-2)","Either, argued from the criteria. Buckets add weight at the feet, which resists lifting but lets the frame lean. Guy ropes out to pegs hold the top against leaning and lifting, which is where the wind pushes hardest. A student who says use both is reasoning well. (3-5-ETS1-2)","Any idea in the materials given: open one side of the cloth so wind passes through instead of pushing on a full sail, lower the canopy so it catches less wind, set the stall so the open side faces away from the wind, tie the frame to the stall itself, run a rope over the top and peg both ends. (3-5-ETS1-2)"]},{std:["3-5-ETS1-3"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"planning a fair test with controlled variables and naming a failure point",html:()=>`<p class="big">The crew wants to find out whether the sand buckets or the guy ropes hold the canopy better. They have two identical canopies and a large fan.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("wind",96)}${F.icon("tent",96)}</div>`,qs:["Plan the test. What will you measure?","Name two things you must keep the same for both canopies so the test is fair.","Name one failure point: where your fix is most likely to give way first."],key:["Any measured outcome: how far each canopy shifts in centimeters, the fan setting at which a leg first lifts, how long each one stands at the highest setting. A number, not an impression. (3-5-ETS1-3)","Any two: the same fan at the same distance and setting, canopies facing the same way, the same ground, the same cloth tightness, the same length of time. Only the hold down method should differ. (3-5-ETS1-3)","Any sensible weak spot: a bucket tips or its rope slips off the leg, a peg pulls out of soft ground, the cloth tears at a corner where the rope pulls, a leg bends, the knot works loose in gusts. (3-5-ETS1-3)"]}]},{id:28,title:"Fairs Across the Regions",short:"Regions",parts:[{std:["HSS-4.1"],n:4,dur:90,dir:"Study the chart of four county fairs. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"describing the physical and human geographic features that define places and regions in California",html:()=>`<p class="big">Four county fairs in California, and the land each county sits on.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("wheat",80)}${F.icon("mountain",80)}${F.icon("wave",80)}${F.icon("cactus",80)}</div>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>County fair</th><th>Region</th><th>Height of the fairground</th><th>What is shown and judged</th></tr>
     <tr><td>A valley county</td><td>Central Valley</td><td>25 m</td><td>almonds, tomatoes, dairy cattle, tractors</td></tr>
     <tr><td>A north coast county</td><td>Coast</td><td>15 m</td><td>dairy, timber, apples, fishing gear</td></tr>
     <tr><td>A Sierra county</td><td>Mountains</td><td>1,100 m</td><td>timber, cattle from summer pasture, gold panning displays</td></tr>
     <tr><td>A desert county</td><td>Desert</td><td>700 m</td><td>dates, solar power displays, horses</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Each fair shows different things. Explain how the region sets what is shown, using two of the fairs.","Two fairs show timber. What do those two regions have in common that the other two do not?","The Sierra county fair has gold panning displays. Explain why that county and not the desert county.","Name one physical feature and one human feature from this chart. Then say what makes each one that kind."],key:["Any two explained: the valley county has deep soil and irrigation so it shows almonds, tomatoes and dairy; the coast county has rain and forest so it shows dairy, timber and apples, and the ocean gives it fishing gear; the desert county is hot and dry with strong sun so it shows dates and solar power. Partial: one fair explained is approaching, and two fairs named with no link to their regions is approaching too. (HSS-4.1)","Enough rain and cool enough summers to grow large trees. The coast gets ocean rain and the mountains get rain and snow. The valley is too dry in summer and the desert far too dry for forest. Partial: naming the rain with nothing said about why the other two regions cannot grow forest is approaching. (HSS-4.1)","The gold was found in the Sierra Nevada foothills and worked in the mountain rivers, so that history belongs to that county. The desert county's history is of dates, ranching and more recently solar power. Partial: naming the Sierra with nothing said about the desert county is approaching. (HSS-4.1)","Physical: the regions, the elevations, the climate. Human: the fairgrounds, the tractors, the solar displays, the fishing gear, the irrigation behind the valley crops. Physical comes from nature, human is built or done by people. Partial: one of each named with no difference stated is approaching. (HSS-4.1)"]}]},{id:29,title:"Gatherings and Gold",short:"California Past",parts:[{std:["HSS-4.2"],n:3,dur:145,dir:"Read the chart, then answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"describing the social and economic life and the interactions among the peoples of early California",html:()=>`<p class="big">When people in early California gathered, and what the gathering was for.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Period</th><th>When people gathered</th><th>What happened there</th></tr>
     <tr><td>First peoples, for thousands of years</td><td>after the autumn acorn harvest, and at set trading times</td><td>neighboring villages and nations met to trade shell beads, obsidian and food, to settle matters between them, to play games and dance, and to hold the ceremonies each nation kept, which were not the same from nation to nation</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Spanish mission period, 1769 onward</td><td>when a supply ship arrived, after the grain harvest, and every day for the lessons and services the Franciscans held</td><td>goods were unloaded and shared out among the missions, the year's crop was counted and stored, and Native Californians who had been brought to the mission were taught Christianity and Spanish trades</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Mexican rancho period, 1820s onward</td><td>at the spring roundup, and when trading ships called</td><td>neighboring ranchos worked the herds together, held horse riding contests, and traded hides for goods from the ships</td></tr></table>`,qs:["According to the chart, what were the Franciscans at the missions teaching the Native Californians who had been brought there? Then name what was traded in each of the three periods.","Neighboring ranchos worked the herds together at the roundup. Why would separate ranchos need to do that?","The first peoples used gatherings to settle matters between nations and to hold their own ceremonies. What does that tell you about how they dealt with one another. What does it tell you about where ceremony sat in a nation's year?"],key:["Christianity and Spanish trades. Trading: first peoples, shell beads, obsidian and food between villages and nations; missions, supplies brought by ship and shared among the missions; ranchos, cattle hides and tallow traded to ships for goods. Both halves are needed for full credit. Partial: one half without the other is approaching, so the teaching named with only one period's trade sits there. (HSS-4.2)","Cattle grazed on open range and the herds of neighboring ranchos mixed together, so the only way to sort and brand them was for everyone to gather and work them at the same time. Partial: saying it was easier together with nothing said about the herds mixing is approaching. (HSS-4.2)","They had settled ways of handling disagreements without fighting, and regular times and places for doing it. That is a form of law and diplomacy, carried out at a gathering rather than in a courthouse. On ceremony: it was held at the same gatherings as the trading and the law, at set times in the year, so religious life was part of the ordinary working year rather than separate from it. Each nation kept its own ceremonies rather than a shared religion. Both halves are needed for full credit. Partial: one half without the other is approaching, so the law and diplomacy half with nothing on ceremony sits there. (HSS-4.2)"]},{std:["HSS-4.3"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study the events, then answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"explaining the economic, social and political life of California from the Bear Flag Republic to statehood",html:()=>`<p class="big">Five events, listed out of order.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Letter</th><th>Event</th></tr>
     <tr><td>P</td><td>California is admitted as the 31st state.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Q</td><td>Settlers at Sonoma declare the Bear Flag Republic.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>R</td><td>Delegates meet at Monterey and write a state constitution.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>S</td><td>Gold is found at Coloma on the American River.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>T</td><td>The war between the United States and Mexico ends and California passes to the United States.</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Put the five events in order and give the year of each one you know.","Explain the chain between event S and event P.","Event Q lasted only weeks. What ended it, and what does that tell you about who held power in California in 1846?"],key:["Q in 1846, then T and S both in 1848 with the war ending and gold found the same year, then R in 1849, then P in 1850. Accept either order for T and S with an explanation. Partial: the five in the right order with no years is approaching. (HSS-4.3)","Gold brought tens of thousands of people in about a year, which created an urgent need for laws, courts and government, so delegates wrote a constitution in 1849 and California was admitted as a state in 1850. The gold is what made statehood come so quickly. Partial: naming the population rise with no step through the constitution to statehood is approaching. (HSS-4.3)","The war between the United States and Mexico reached California, and California came under the United States instead. It shows that in 1846 power in California was being decided by two national governments at war, not by the settlers who raised the flag. Partial: naming the war with nothing said about who held power is approaching. (HSS-4.3)"]}]},{id:30,title:"A Farm State and Its Governments",short:"Government",parts:[{std:["HSS-4.4"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study the chart, then answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"tracing how California became an agricultural and industrial power since the 1850s",html:()=>`<p class="big">California farming, then and now.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Then and now</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1860s main crop</td><td>wheat, grown without irrigation and shipped as grain</td></tr>
     <tr><td>What changed</td><td>canals and aqueducts brought water, and refrigerated railcars and later trucks carried fresh produce</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Today</td><td>California grows more than a third of the vegetables and about three quarters of the fruit and nuts grown in the United States</td></tr>
     <tr><td>What it depends on</td><td>stored mountain water, a long warm growing season, deep valley soil, and a large workforce</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Industry too</td><td>ports, trucking, packing houses, canneries and equipment makers all grew around the farms</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Why did California move away from wheat toward fruit, nuts and vegetables?","Name two things today's farming depends on that were not needed for 1860s wheat.","Explain how farming growing bigger also made other industries grow."],key:["Water brought by canals made it possible to grow crops that need watering all summer, and cooled railcars and trucks made it possible to sell fruit and vegetables far away. Those crops are worth far more than wheat, so once they could be watered and shipped, growers changed. Partial: one of the water and the shipping named is approaching. (HSS-4.4)","Any two: stored mountain water carried by canals, cooling and fast transport, a large workforce for picking and packing, packing houses and canneries. Dry farmed wheat needed none of those. Partial: one thing named is approaching, and two named with no contrast to dry farmed wheat is approaching too. (HSS-4.4)","More crops needed more of everything around them: packing houses and canneries to prepare them, railways, trucks and ports to move them, and makers of tractors, pumps and pipe to supply the farms. Each of those is an industry that grew because farming grew. Partial: one other industry named is approaching. (HSS-4.4)"]},{std:["HSS-4.5"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study the chart, then answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"explaining the structures, functions and powers of local, state and federal government",html:()=>`<p class="big">Who has a part in running a county fair.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Who</th><th>Level</th><th>What they do about the fair</th></tr>
     <tr><td>County board of supervisors</td><td>local</td><td>provides the land, roads and sheriff's patrols</td></tr>
     <tr><td>City council of the host town</td><td>local</td><td>street closures, parking rules, rubbish collection</td></tr>
     <tr><td>State fair board and state department of agriculture</td><td>state</td><td>sets rules for livestock shows and inspects animals for disease</td></tr>
     <tr><td>State legislature</td><td>state</td><td>passes the laws and the budget the fair system runs under</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Federal government</td><td>federal</td><td>rules for moving animals across state lines</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Name the three levels of government in this chart and one thing each one does about the fair.","Why would rules about moving animals across state lines be federal rather than state?","The fair wants a street closed for a parade. Which row of the chart do they go to, and why that one?"],key:["Local: the county provides land, roads and patrols, and the city handles street closures and rubbish. State: the fair board and agriculture department set livestock rules and inspect animals, and the legislature passes the laws and budget. Federal: rules for moving animals between states. Partial: two of the three levels matched with something they do is approaching. (HSS-4.5)","Moving animals between states crosses state borders, so no single state can set the rule for the whole journey. A disease carried across a border affects every state, which makes it a national matter. Partial: saying it is a federal job with no reason is approaching. (HSS-4.5)","The city council of the host town, because street closures and parking in town are the city's responsibility. A student who adds that the county would be asked if the route uses a county road is correct. Partial: the right row named with no reason is approaching. (HSS-4.5)"]}]}];const WC=FLU.split(/\s+/).filter(Boolean).length;const readPart=STOPS[0].parts[0];readPart.n=WC;readPart.key[0]=readPart.key[0].replace("__WC__",WC);readPart.desc=`a ${WC}-word grade-level text read aloud`;const LEVELUPS={2:{id:"L2",title:"Level Up: Bigger or Smaller",std:["5.NF.B.5"],needs:["4.OA.A.1"],n:3,dir:"Answer out loud without working the whole problem out. Tell how you decided.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Without multiplying, is 48 × 3/4 greater than 48 or less than 48? How do you know?</li><li>Without multiplying, is 20 × 5/4 greater than 20 or less than 20? How do you know?</li><li>A banner is 12 feet long. A second banner is 2/3 as long. Is the second banner longer or shorter, and how long is it?</li></ol>`,key:["Less than 48. Multiplying by a fraction smaller than 1 scales the number down, and 3/4 is less than 1. (5.NF.B.5b, interpreting multiplication as scaling)","Greater than 20. 5/4 is greater than 1, so multiplying by it scales the number up. (5.NF.B.5b)","Shorter, and 8 feet long. 2/3 is less than 1, so the second banner is shorter, and 2/3 × 12 = 8. (5.NF.B.5b, scaling in a real situation)"]},4:{id:"L4",title:"Level Up: Exact Words",std:["RL.5.1"],needs:["RL.4.1"],n:2,dir:"Find the words in the story and read them out loud exactly as they are written.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Read me the exact words that prove Ibrahim checked the total more than twice.</li><li>The story never says Ibrahim told the truth even though it was hard. Read me the exact words that show it anyway.</li></ol>${F.passage(BOX,BOX_TITLE,true)}`,key:["Quotes accurately, such as: “He counted again and got ninety-four again,” and “He went through the box twice more.” The student should read the words as written, not retell them. (RL.5.1, quoting accurately)","Quotes: “He did not tell anybody for eleven minutes,” and “told her the whole thing, numbers and all, and his voice was not steady.” Together they show he found it hard and told her all of it anyway. (RL.5.1, quoting to back an inference)"]},5:{id:"L5",title:"Level Up: Unlike Bottoms",std:["5.NF.A.1"],needs:["4.NF.A.1"],n:3,dir:"Solve on your paper and hold it up. Show the equivalent fractions you used.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>1/2 + 1/3</li><li>3/4 − 2/5</li><li>Two trays are the same size. 2/3 of one is left and 1/4 of the other is left. How much is left altogether?</li></ol>`,key:["5/6. In sixths, 1/2 is 3/6 and 1/3 is 2/6, and 3/6 + 2/6 = 5/6. (5.NF.A.1, adding fractions with unlike denominators)","7/20. In twentieths, 3/4 is 15/20 and 2/5 is 8/20, and 15/20 − 8/20 = 7/20. (5.NF.A.1, subtracting fractions with unlike denominators)","11/12 of a tray. In twelfths, 2/3 is 8/12 and 1/4 is 3/12, and 8/12 + 3/12 = 11/12. (5.NF.A.1, a word problem with unlike denominators)"]},9:{id:"L9",title:"Level Up: Two Steps of Units",std:["5.MD.A.1"],needs:["4.MD.A.1"],n:3,dir:"Solve on your paper and hold it up.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>A roll of ribbon is 450 centimeters long. How many meters is that?</li><li>The crew has three pieces of cloth: one 3 meters, one 180 centimeters, and one 2.5 meters. What is the total length in meters?</li><li>A pot holds 4,500 milliliters of lemonade. How many liters is that?</li></ol>`,key:["4.5 meters. 450 ÷ 100 = 4.5. (5.MD.A.1, converting a smaller unit to a larger one)","7.3 meters. 180 centimeters is 1.8 meters, and 3 + 1.8 + 2.5 = 7.3. (5.MD.A.1, a multi-step problem that needs a conversion first)","4.5 liters. 4,500 ÷ 1,000 = 4.5. (5.MD.A.1)"]},10:{id:"L10",title:"Level Up: Paired Words",std:["L.5.1"],needs:["L.4.1"],n:3,dir:"Say each sentence out loud the correct way.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Finish it with either and or, or with neither and nor: ____ the tape ____ the spare cord was in the crate. The crate was empty.</li><li>By the time the gate opened, the crew ____ for three hours. (work) Start with had been.</li><li>Fix the shift in tense: Yesterday the crew cut the grass, walks the field, and carries the tables out.</li></ol>`,key:["Neither the tape nor the spare cord was in the crate. (L.5.1, correlative conjunctions)","had been working. (L.5.1, perfect verb tense)","Yesterday the crew cut the grass, walked the field, and carried the tables out. (L.5.1, correcting an inappropriate shift in verb tense)"]},12:{id:"L12",title:"Level Up: Shapes Inside Shapes",std:["5.G.B.4"],needs:["4.G.A.2"],n:3,dir:"Answer out loud. Explain each answer with a property, not with how the shape looks.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Is every rectangle a parallelogram? Is every parallelogram a rectangle? Explain both answers.</li><li>Shape A is a rectangle. Name two other categories Shape A belongs to, and say why.</li><li>Is a square a rhombus? Is a trapezoid a parallelogram? Explain.</li></ol>${shapeRow()}`,key:["Every rectangle is a parallelogram, because a rectangle has two pairs of parallel sides, which is what makes a parallelogram. Not every parallelogram is a rectangle, because a parallelogram does not have to have right angles, and Shape D is the example. (5.G.B.4, classifying figures in a hierarchy)","Any two correct, with the reason: a parallelogram, because its opposite sides are parallel; a quadrilateral, because it has four sides; and a polygon, because it is a closed figure made of straight sides. (5.G.B.4)","A square is a rhombus, because a rhombus is a quadrilateral with four equal sides and a square has four equal sides. A trapezoid is not a parallelogram, because it has only one pair of parallel sides and a parallelogram needs two. Shape C is the trapezoid. (5.G.B.4)"]},14:{id:"L14",title:"Level Up: Where It Came From",std:["W.5.8"],needs:["W.4.8"],n:3,dir:"Work on your paper and hold it up.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Take one fact from Source 2 and write it in your own words, with the source named in your sentence.</li><li>Now quote the same source exactly, with quotation marks and the author's name.</li><li>The two sources do not say the same things. Write one sentence that uses a fact from each one, and show which fact came from which source.</li></ol>${twoSources()}`,key:["A paraphrase that keeps the meaning but changes the wording, with the source named, such as: Yusuf Carrington writes that most sunken cakes come from opening the oven too soon or from using too much baking powder. (W.5.8, summarizing or paraphrasing and providing the source)","An exact quotation inside quotation marks with the author named, such as: Yusuf Carrington writes, “Nine sunken cakes out of ten come from the same two mistakes.” The words inside the marks must match the source exactly. (W.5.8, quoting)","One sentence drawing on both, with each fact attributed, such as: Delia Marchetti explains that baking powder holds an acid and a base that react in water, while Yusuf Carrington warns that using too much of it makes bubbles so large that the walls tear. (W.5.8, drawing on several sources)"]}};const PASSAGE_STD=["RL.4.1","RL.4.4","RI.4.1","W.4.8","RL.5.1","W.5.8","RI.4.9"];const MULT_N=12;let multSel=[0,0];const STARTERS={"W.4.8":["Source ____ says ____.","Gas: ____ (Source ____)","Sinking: ____ (Source ____)","My sources: 1. ____ 2. ____"],"W.5.8":["According to ____, ____.","____ writes, “____.”","Source 1 says ____, while Source 2 says ____."],"SL.4.2":["In my own words, ____.","The report said that ____.","The rule is ____.","The table shows ____."],"SL.4.4":["This year I worked on ____.","I started by ____.","The hardest part was ____.","Next time I would ____."],"RL.4.1":["The story says ____.","I know ____ because the story says ____.","Two details are ____ and ____."],"RI.4.1":["The article says ____.","I know ____ because the article says ____."],"RL.4.4":["____ means ____.","I know because the story says ____.","This is a simile or a metaphor because ____."],"RL.5.1":["The story says, “____.”","The words “____” show that ____."],"W.4.1":["I think the fair should ____.","One reason is ____.","For example, ____.","That is why ____."],"W.4.2":["Setting up a fair means ____.","First, ____.","____ means ____.","Now you know ____."],"W.4.3":["It was the day of ____.","Then ____.","“____,” said ____.","In the end ____."],"RI.4.9":["Source 1 says ____.","Source 2 says ____.","Put together, they show ____."]};function multHtml(){let h=`<div class="tool-scroll"><table class="mult"><tr><th>×</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++)h+=`<th data-r="0" data-c="${c}" class="${multSel[1]===c?"hl":""}">${c}</th>`;h+="</tr>";for(let r=1;r<=MULT_N;r++){h+=`<tr><th data-r="${r}" data-c="0" class="${multSel[0]===r?"hl":""}">${r}</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++){const hr=multSel[0]===r,hc=multSel[1]===c;h+=`<td data-r="${r}" data-c="${c}" class="${hr&&hc?"pick":hr||hc?"hl":""}">${r*c}</td>`}h+="</tr>"}return h+`</table></div><p class="small">Click a number to light up its row and column. Click it again to clear.</p>`}function unitHtml(){return F.table(["1 of the bigger unit","equals","of the smaller unit"],[["1 kilometer","=","1,000 meters"],["1 meter","=","100 centimeters"],["1 kilogram","=","1,000 grams"],["1 pound","=","16 ounces"],["1 liter","=","1,000 milliliters"],["1 hour","=","60 minutes"],["1 minute","=","60 seconds"],["1 foot","=","12 inches"],["1 yard","=","3 feet"]])+`<p class="small">Going from a bigger unit to a smaller one, multiply, because each unit is smaller so it takes more of them. Going the other way, divide.</p>`}function fracHtml(){return F.table(["What to do","How"],[["Make an equivalent fraction","multiply the top and the bottom by the same number, or divide them both by the same number"],["Compare two fractions","make the bottoms the same, then compare the tops"],["Compare using a benchmark","ask whether each one is less than, equal to, or more than 1/2"],["Record the answer","write it with >, <, or ="]])+`<p class="small">Two fractions can only be compared when they are parts of the same size whole.</p>`}function decHtml(){return F.table(["Decimal","In words","Out of 100"],[["0.3","three tenths","30 hundredths"],["0.30","thirty hundredths","30 hundredths"],["0.37","thirty-seven hundredths","37 hundredths"],["0.4","four tenths","40 hundredths"],["0.09","nine hundredths","9 hundredths"]])+`<p class="small">To compare two decimals, write them both in hundredths first. One tenth is the same as ten hundredths.</p>`}function shapeHtml(){return F.table(["Word","What it means"],[["Parallel sides","run in the same direction and never meet, no matter how far they go"],["Perpendicular sides","meet at a square corner"],["Right angle","a square corner, like the corner of a card"],["Right triangle","a triangle with one right angle"],["Parallelogram","four sides, two pairs of parallel sides"],["Trapezoid","four sides, exactly one pair of parallel sides"],["Rhombus","four sides, all four the same length"]])+`<p class="small">Use the corner of a card to test whether an angle is a right angle, smaller, or bigger.</p>`}function wordPartHtml(){return F.table(["Word part","What it does"],[["re-","again or back: rehearsal"],["ad-","to or toward: admission"],["con-","with or together: contribute"],["in-","not, or into: incredible"],["-ate","to do: decorate"],["-ment","the act or result of: arrangement"],["-ous","full of: generous"],["-ible, -able","can be: incredible"],["-ary","having to do with: temporary"],["-ly","in that way: approximately"]])+`<p class="small">Cover the beginning and the ending, read the middle, then put the whole word back together and say it smoothly.</p>`}const SENT_ALIAS={opinion:"W.4.1",facts:"W.4.2",story:"W.4.3","W.4.1":"opinion","W.4.2":"facts","W.4.3":"story"};function sentKey(st){const ps=st&&st.parts&&st.parts.length?st.parts:st?[st]:[];const pick=c=>!c?null:STARTERS[c]?c:SENT_ALIAS[c]&&STARTERS[SENT_ALIAS[c]]?SENT_ALIAS[c]:null;for(const p of ps){const a=pick(p.alt&&p.alt.key||p.wk||p.wt);if(a)return a;for(const c of p.std||[]){const k=pick(c);if(k)return k}}return null}function sentHtml(st){const k=sentKey(st);return`<ul class="starters">${(STARTERS[k]||STARTERS["W.4.8"]).map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`}const TOOLS={mult:{name:"Multiplication chart",std:["4.OA.A.1","4.MD.A.1","4.NF.A.1","4.NF.A.2","5.NF.A.1","5.NF.B.5","5.MD.A.1","4.NBT.A.1","4.MD.A.3"],html:multHtml,bind:(b,r)=>b.querySelectorAll("[data-r]").forEach(c=>c.onclick=()=>{const rr=+c.dataset.r,k=+c.dataset.c;multSel=multSel[0]===rr&&multSel[1]===k?[0,0]:[rr,k];r()})},units:{name:"Measurement unit card",std:["4.MD.A.1","5.MD.A.1"],html:unitHtml},frac:{name:"Fraction card",std:["4.NF.A.1","4.NF.A.2","5.NF.A.1","5.NF.B.5"],html:fracHtml},dec:{name:"Decimal place card",std:["4.NF.C.7","4.NF.C.6"],html:decHtml},shapes:{name:"Shape word card",std:["4.G.A.2","5.G.B.4"],html:shapeHtml},parts:{name:"Word part chart",std:["RF.4.3"],html:wordPartHtml},sent:{name:"Sentence frames",std:Object.keys(STARTERS),html:sentHtml}};const SUP_CAVEATS=[{t:"mult",std:["4.OA.A.1"],msg:"The multiplication chart supplies the facts, so rate on whether the student can say the comparison in words and write it as an equation, not on the fact itself."},{t:"mult",std:["4.MD.A.1","5.MD.A.1"],msg:"The multiplication chart gives the products. Rate on whether the student picks the right operation and the right unit."},{t:"mult",std:["4.NF.A.1","4.NF.A.2","5.NF.A.1","5.NF.B.5"],msg:"The chart gives the products used to build equivalent fractions. Rate on whether the student knows what to multiply and why."},{t:"units",std:["4.MD.A.1","5.MD.A.1"],msg:"The unit card gives every equivalence this stop asks for, so the stop then shows only whether the student can pick the right operation. Note it on the record sheet."},{t:"frac",std:["4.NF.A.1"],msg:"The fraction card states the rule for making an equivalent fraction, so count the generating questions as supported and rate the explanation of why it works."},{t:"frac",std:["4.NF.A.2"],msg:"The card gives both comparing methods and the benchmark idea, so count the comparisons as supported, not independent."},{t:"frac",std:["5.NF.A.1","5.NF.B.5"],msg:"The card names the method. Rate on whether the student carries it out and can say what the answer means."},{t:"dec",std:["4.NF.C.7"],msg:"The decimal card shows each decimal written in hundredths, which is the reasoning step this standard asks for. Count those comparisons as supported."},{t:"shapes",std:["4.G.A.2","5.G.B.4"],msg:"The shape word card defines parallel, perpendicular, right angle, parallelogram, trapezoid, and rhombus, so the naming questions become supported. Rate on whether the student can find the features on the shapes."},{t:"parts",std:["RF.4.3"],msg:"The word part chart gives the meaning of the prefixes and endings on the cards, so count the word-part part of this stop as supported and rate the reading of the whole word."},{t:"sent",std:["W.4.8","W.5.8"],msg:"The frames show how to label a note and name a source, so count the source list as supported, not independent."},{t:"sent",std:["SL.4.2"],msg:"The frames prompt the student to start with their own words, so count the paraphrasing cue as supported and rate on whether the content is right and genuinely reworded."},{t:"sent",std:["SL.4.4"],msg:"The frames supply the order of the report, so count the organization check as supported and rate the facts and details the student adds."},{t:"sent",std:["RL.4.1","RI.4.1","RL.4.4","RL.5.1"],msg:"The frames remind the student to point back at the text. Rate on whether the detail chosen is the right one."},{t:"passage",std:PASSAGE_STD,msg:"If the passage was read to the student, this stop shows listening comprehension, not reading."},{t:"mult",std:["4.NBT.A.1"],msg:"The multiplication chart shows the ten times row, which is the whole idea of this check. Note that the ten times relationship was read off the chart."},{t:"mult",std:["4.MD.A.3"],msg:"The chart supplies the products and the matching division facts. Rate on whether the student picks area or perimeter, uses square units for area, and can say why dividing finds the missing side."},{t:"dec",std:["4.NF.C.6"],msg:"The decimal card already pairs each decimal with its name and its hundredths form, which is most of what this check asks. Count these as supported."},{t:"sent",std:["W.4.1","W.4.2","W.4.3"],msg:"The frames supply the opening, the linking words, and the closing, so count those checks as supported, not independent."},{t:"sent",std:["RI.4.9"],msg:"The frames hand the student the shape of an answer that uses both sources. Rate on which facts are chosen and on whether the conclusion is really drawn from both."}];return{stops:STOPS,levelups:LEVELUPS,tools:TOOLS,supCaveats:SUP_CAVEATS,passageStd:PASSAGE_STD}}});
